00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:07.590 align:start position:0%
 
vaccine passports are coming  someday soon you may need to prove your  covert status  to go to the pub travel abroad or even  go to work governments hope  these passes could be key to  kick-starting the economy  and restoring basic freedoms but could  this come  at a cost to individuals  the weekend is underway in tel aviv in  israel  restaurants here only recently reopened  from lockdown  but covert 19 feels like a distant  memory  the reason the green pass an app  that lets you prove you've had the jab  [Music]  you know as a chef it's it's made me  happy like  it's a huge joy we feel like uh we see  colors again israel is winning the  global vaccination race  most over 40s are now fully vaccinated  and if you've had both jabs the green  pass opens doors  giving you access to restaurants hotels  and gyms i saw a lot of young people  that it was for them the incentive to do  it  they wanted to come back to life  actually more than a paper  green paper just come back to life  the green pass might be new but vaccine  passports are not  travellers to regions with yellow fever  already need to carry a vaccination  certificate  and children attending public school in  america must be vaccinated for several  diseases  but governments are now paying keen  attention to schemes like the green pass  eu leaders have agreed to develop a  covet 19 vaccine digital  vaccine passports that could to see if  they could be  key to lifting lockdowns  but how well would they actually work  vaccine passports might not be that  effective for two reasons  first although vaccines prevent serious  illness  it's not yet clear how effectively they  stop the spread  of covert 19 particularly for new  variants  so vaccinated people could potentially  still pass covid  on to others second  vaccine passports are only useful for a  very limited window domestically  they're not very useful early on because  there's not many people who benefit  and they're not very useful late on  either because vaccines work in two ways  they work by protecting you by priming  your immune system  so that if you meet an infection you're  ready to fight it off  but they also work by making sure that  you mostly won't meet infection because  other people around you are vaccinated  too  if as in israel most people have been  vaccinated  then the chances of meeting an infected  person are low  so a vaccine passport adds little  additional protection  however in places that are taking longer  to vaccinate  like the eu these passports could be  useful for much  longer to help reopen the economy  [Music]  but could cobid passports create  inequality  [Music]  most countries are vaccinating old  people first  and some think vaccine passports could  risk creating a two-tiered society  where vaccinated seniors can resume  normal life  while the young are stuck inside waiting  for the jab  so the vaccine passport is quite a  temporary thing that maybe governments  just use as one tool as they exit  lockdown  if the government is quick and fair and  transparent about it i think that sort  of issue of unfairness  will seem reasonable it's unfairness for  a bigger fairness  in israel relatively few under-16s have  had the jab  so they can't go inside restaurants  but most seem willing to make this small  sacrifice  or not eat out at all if it's too cold  we got yesterday uh like seven  uh guests that one of them was like 16  years old  and it was pretty cold outside and they  asked they understand they said with the  coat and  all good you know we we have to protect  each other  a bigger sticking point is jobs  some businesses from care homes to  plumbing firms  have already said they'll require proof  of vaccination from new employees  we're just going to make it a  requirement it's going to be important  for us to protect customers and our  members of staff  this could make it harder for the  unvaccinated to find  work it's already the case that we  require people in certain sensitive jobs  for example in the armed forces or  working in certain healthcare settings  to have all their vaccines  so i don't think it would be  unreasonable or surprising that covid  got added to that list  what we would worry about is if this  became just a requirement to do any job  [Music]  this would be especially problematic as  some groups are much less likely to have  had the jab than others  across 39 states in america the  vaccination  rate among white people is over twice as  high as the rate for hispanic people  and nearly twice as high as the rate for  black people  this may be because black americans are  on average  younger than whites meaning they're  further back in the vaccine queue  they're also less likely to trust the  vaccine because of a long history of  misuse of health data  and bad vaccine programs where i think  you might worry about  entrenching inequality in the longer  term is if this sort of scheme  becomes a power grab and ends up being  something that allows health data to be  used  to govern access to public spaces and to  work places  but this could be avoided if cobit  passports are a temporary measure  and restricted to certain key  professions  there are also concerns over freedom of  choice  back in tel aviv you need proof of  vaccination to access  many aspects of normal life and some  people  like mikhail feel they had no choice but  to have the jab  i'm a healthy 41 year old woman i don't  think  i needed the vaccination against kovid  the only reason  why i got the vaccination was not for  health reasons  it was just because if you're not  vaccinated you can't go places you can't  go  to the gym you can leave the country  if covered passports were to be rolled  out many people could face stark choices  like these  in return for short-term freedoms rather  than feeling  forced to have the jab people should  have access to information that allows  them to make an informed decision  the economist has built an interactive  covert 19 risk estimator  to help with these decisions you can try  it yourself  at the link above perhaps  one of the biggest concerns around  covert passports is  data privacy a vaccine passport is based  on  sensitive health data and some worry  this could open the door to allowing  governments to snoop on their citizens  take singapore the government promised  data gathered by its contact tracing app  would only be used to stop the spread of  covert 19  but in january they admitted the data  had been shared with the police to fight  crime  do we really want to live in a world  where government agencies  are able just to see where you were you  could imagine all sorts of bad  uses for that data singapore's  government said the data sharing was a  mistake  and developers working on vaccine  passports are confident they will be  designed in a way that will share  minimal personal data  one area where development is racing  ahead is international travel  there are already several digital health  apps in development  that allow people to prove their covert  free and in time  vaccinated if you want people to be able  to travel between countries that are  incredibly different stages in their  journey towards conquering coverage  you might decide that you only allow  people to cross borders if you could  think of them as covert safe  countries like israel america and  britain may be racing towards herd  immunity  but large parts of africa may not get  the jab for at least the next two years  a covered passport would let vaccinated  people cross borders and provide a  welcome boost to crippled economies  countries like greece have already said  they will welcome  vaccinated brits this summer but  tourists shouldn't pack their bags  just yet a third wave of the virus is  sweeping much of europe  and vaccinated holidaymakers could still  cause local  outbreaks of disease any tourists who  arrive  even if they're vaccinated they're going  to be a reason that unvaccinated greeks  are coming out and mingling and that  will increase the number of cases it's  really a trade-off it's a trade-off here  between  getting the economy going again and  increasing the number of cases  governments need to be really really  transparent about that to explain to  people  this is the hard choice that's in front  of us what do you think what are the  trade-offs that you would find  acceptable  israel's economy is predicted to grow by  up to 7.5 percent this year if the  vaccination program continues at its  current rate but it remains to be seen  how long  this green pass effect will last  vaccine passports are just one of many  useful tools to help  restart the global economy and although  developers already overcoming concerns  about privacy and security  their domestic success will depend on  how well the vaccines themselves prevent  transmission of covert 19  and how quickly countries can reach herd  immunity  i'm helen joyce deputy foreign editor at  the economist  you can read my reporting on vaccine  passports by clicking on the link  opposite thank you for watching and  don't forget to subscribe  you

